.TH SIEGESEAL 1 "2026-11-19" "SiegeSeal 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
siegeseal \- sign files and verify signed letters with Ed25519
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B siegeseal keygen
.I name
.br
.B siegeseal fingerprint
.I name.pub
.br
.B siegeseal sign
.I name.seal file
.br
.B siegeseal verify
.I name.pub file
.RI [ file.sig ]
.br
.B siegeseal verify-envelope
.I sender.pub envelope.hex
.br
.B siegeseal
.RB [ \-\-version | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B siegeseal
creates Ed25519 key pairs, signs files, and checks signatures, so a reader can
prove who wrote a file and that nobody has changed it since. It also checks
letters exported from the SiegeSeal Classroom web app.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI keygen " name"
Write
.IB name .seal
(the private key, created with mode 600) and
.IB name .pub
(the public key), and print the key's fingerprint. Refuses to overwrite an
existing private key.
.TP
.BI fingerprint " name.pub"
Print the short fingerprint of a public key, for comparing in person.
.TP
.BI sign " name.seal file"
Sign
.I file
and write the signature to
.IB file .sig .
.TP
.BI verify " name.pub file \fR[\fPfile.sig\fR]\fP"
Check that
.I file
was signed with the private key matching
.I name.pub
and has not changed since.
.TP
.BI verify-envelope " sender.pub envelope.hex"
Decode a letter exported from the web app, print its fields and digest, and
check its seal against the sender's public key.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; for
.B verify
and
.BR verify-envelope ,
the signature is genuine.
.TP
.B 1
The signature does not match: the file was changed, or it was signed by someone else.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, unreadable file, or malformed key.
.SH FILES
Key and signature files are one line of text:
.IR "kind name hex" ,
where kind is
.BR siegeseal-secret ,
.B siegeseal-public
or
.BR siegeseal-signature .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
siegeseal keygen aurelia
siegeseal sign aurelia.seal orders.txt
siegeseal verify aurelia.pub orders.txt && echo genuine
.fi
.SH SECURITY
Keep the
.B .seal
file private: anyone who has it can sign as you. A signature proves only that
the holder of the matching private key signed; compare fingerprints in person
before trusting a public key. Signing does not encrypt: anyone can read a signed file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR siege_sim (1)
